 An object patch merges member by member and `null` removes a member; any other patch (including arrays and `null`)
 replaces the target. A non-object target is treated as `{}` when the patch is an object. It never throws. As the RFC
 notes, `null` cannot be set as a member value. `__proto__`, `constructor` and `prototype` are ordinary member names.
+
+## Creating a JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+```ts
+import { createPatch } from "json-toolkit";
+
+createPatch({ a: 1, b: [1, 2] }, { a: 2, b: [1, 2, 3] });
+// [{ op: "replace", path: "/a", value: 2 }, { op: "add", path: "/b/2", value: 3 }]
+```
+
+`createPatch(from, to)` returns a patch such that `applyPatch(from, patch)` equals `to` (JSON equality, RFC 6902 §4.6);
+equal documents give `[]`. Only changes are described: objects are diffed member by member and recursively; arrays are
+aligned by longest common subsequence, so an inserted or removed item is a single `add`/`remove`, a moved item is a `remove`
+plus an `add` (never a rewrite of the items in between), and a changed item is diffed in place. Values of different type
+or differing scalars are `replace`d (root path `""`). Array indices follow the sequential semantics of §3. The result is
+not guaranteed to be the globally shortest patch. Neither input is mutated and the patch shares no structure with them.
