+# Spec: createPatch (JSON Patch generation)
+
+Request: `createPatch(from, to)` returns a JSON Patch (RFC 6902) that turns `from` into `to` and describes only what changed.
+
+**Standard:** RFC 6902 §3 (patch is an array of operations applied in order, each on the result of the previous), §4.1–4.4
+(`add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`), §4.6 (equality of JSON values: object member order is irrelevant), and RFC 6901 §3/§5
+(pointer syntax; `~` → `~0`, `/` → `~1` when building paths).
+
+## Behaviour
+- `applyPatch(from, createPatch(from, to))` deep-equals `to`. Equal documents (§4.6) give `[]`.
+- Objects: members only in `from` → `remove`; only in `to` → `add`; in both and different → recurse into the member.
+- Arrays: items are aligned by longest common subsequence (JSON equality). An inserted item → one `add` at its index; a
+  deleted item → one `remove`; neighbouring items are not touched. A moved item → `remove` + `add` (no rewrite of the items
+  between). A changed item at an aligned position (same container type) is diffed recursively, otherwise `replace`d.
+  Indices are those of the array as already modified by earlier operations (§3).
+- Scalars or values of different type (object/array/scalar/null) → `replace`; a root-level change uses path `""`.
+- Neither input is mutated; values in the patch are copies sharing no structure with the inputs. `__proto__`,
+  `constructor`, `prototype` are ordinary member names. Never throws for JSON input.
+- Patch is not guaranteed to be the globally shortest one (array alignment is LCS-based), but never rewrites unchanged parts.
